// Placement of the core's large constant data. Both macros are empty by default:
// the data is plain `const` and lands in .rodata (on the ESP32-S3 that is flash,
// mapped through the flash cache, and costs no internal RAM; the Cardputer build
// defines neither). A platform that needs the data elsewhere defines them on its
// compiler command line, e.g. a section attribute.
//
// PG_TABLE_ATTR: the HOT DSP lookup tables the voices read per sample (lead
// wavetables 38.5 KB, bass sine 2 KB and BLEP 2.2 KB; generated by
// tools/gen/gen_tables.cpp). If profiling on the device shows flash-cache misses
// in the oscillators, the firmware can move them into internal RAM without
// touching this code, e.g. with -DPG_TABLE_ATTR=DRAM_ATTR (ESP-IDF, ~43 KB of
// DRAM for all of them). On the Daisy they belong in fast memory (AXI SRAM or
// DTCM), not in QSPI.
//
// PG_BULK_ATTR: the BIG, COLD constant data, read at note-on rates or slower
// (or sequentially): the ROM sounds (rom_samples_data.cpp, 136.7 KB of audio and
// 1.4 KB of overviews; read per sample while a ROM sound plays, sequentially)
// and the built-in instrument library (builtin_data.cpp kBuiltin, 58.9 KB on
// x86-64; read when an instrument is bound or a macro moves, about 86 Hz per
// moving track). On the Daisy (a 480 KB RAM-booted program) these are what to
// move out, to QSPI flash or SDRAM, e.g.
//   -DPG_BULK_ATTR='__attribute__((section(".qspiflash_data")))'
// (the section name is the platform's linker script's). docs/ARCHITECTURE.md
// "Sample rates" and docs/STATE.md list every large table with its size.
#pragma once

#ifndef PG_TABLE_ATTR
#define PG_TABLE_ATTR
#endif

#ifndef PG_BULK_ATTR
#define PG_BULK_ATTR
#endif
